WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.012
After choosing and filtering rows we often need to
organize the result

00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:10.075
Three useful tools are ORDER BY LIMIT and DISTINCT
ORDER BY controls

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.725
the order of the returned rows In this example
admissions are sorted

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:20.225
by admission_date descending which places more
recent dates first We also add

00:00:20.238 --> 00:00:25.400
admission_id descending as a second sort key A
second key is useful

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.862
when two rows have the same date because it gives
PostgreSQL another

00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:33.300
rule for ordering them LIMIT keeps only a chosen
number of rows

00:00:34.188 --> 00:00:38.800
Here LIMIT three returns only the first three rows
after sorting This

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:43.225
is why ORDER BY and LIMIT often belong together
LIMIT three by

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.525
itself does not mean the three newest records It
only means return

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.650
three rows from whatever order PostgreSQL produces
If the meaning is newest

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:58.650
oldest highest or lowest define that order
explicitly DISTINCT is another tool

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:04.425
It removes repeated result rows For example if you
only wanted a

00:01:04.438 --> 00:01:09.912
list of unique diagnosis values SELECT DISTINCT
diagnosis could be useful Run

00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:14.338
the current query and confirm that only three rows
are returned Then

00:01:14.362 --> 00:01:18.525
try changing LIMIT three to LIMIT five The key idea
is that

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.938
ORDER BY gives meaning to which rows appear first
while LIMIT controls

00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:23.375
how many of those rows you keep
